Essentially I m arguing three things:

<A> Its a bad idea to share critical section and sleep lock with code that can fault. You can use priority inheritance to 
    get the thread holding the lock to get scheduled immediately but you cannot make it complete its critical section any 
    faster.

    Bottomline if you re idea is to share adaptive lock with code that can fault and such is the motivation behind "interrupts
    as threads" stop right there the goal is bad!! :-)

<B> It encourages you to write fat ISRs (as against making you split it into hard/soft parts)

<C> Having a monolithic single ISR as against two halves makes sure that the IRQL remains at this level thereby starving
    even lower IRQL detection until the *ENTIRE* ISR is completed (add to it the worst case lock acquisition time and you 
    get the picture)

==============================
What is a good solution then?
==============================

<a> Have two half ISRs (hard/soft) parts like Linux/Windows.

<b> Hard part is not a thread interrupts below that level are disabled until this is handled. In fact interrupts as such 
    can be disabled until this is handled if need be. In this part you do the minimal lil and queue work.

<c> The queued work part needs to be fair amongst each other (so work queued by IRQL 7 can be surpassed by work queued by
    IRQL 5 based on starvation and interrupt storm)

    Furthermore this queued task can even be supplanted by regular threads based on starvation or moved to some other core
    based on rules of scheduling.

    Furthermore this queued task should be able to block and make most kernel calls (oh yes this should be below the 
    schedulers IRQL level)

I think this solves the "ease of writing ISR" problem (use regular locks) / doesnt hamper lower IRQLs waiting for scheduling
delays (hard ISR is still super fast and not allowed to block) and above all has good concepts of fairness.

Oh and yes you could do coalescing of tasks for example .. IRQL 7 queues a task but doesnt disable its interrupt source (preemptible
IRQL much like our non stall interrupt tree) But before its queued task can be executed that core receives interrupts from IRQL
9. This happens thrice such that there are 3 queued tasks from IRQL 7. "Task coalescing" will allow you to run the task just once.
Of course the coalescing needs to be device aware (so devices sharing the same IRQL shouldnt coalesce :-) that would be fun aint it)


================================
 PREEMPTIBLE ISR
================================

At the start of hard ISR disable your own device interrupt source and reenable it at the end of 
your soft ISR execution. This is what we do today (except for tree 1 ) and is essentially non preemptible.
I think this makes sense for level based interrupts (condition not event)

For edge triggered interrupts (event not condition) it makes sense to be able to queue the separate events.
(really ...? think some more what kind of an ISR would want to queue the same soft ISR again and again and 
possibly take advantage of the OS coalescing them?)



